course: dictionary
lesson: 177
title: Stuck in the shallows
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Aground is a word for ships and boats. It means the boat has hit the bottom of the water in shallow water and cannot move on. «The ship ran aground on the sandbank.» So ran aground means the ship got stuck on sand, rocks or the sea bottom.
explain: Look at the words around it for clues. «The barge runs aground (gets stuck on the bottom) on the other side of the canal.» The brackets tell you the meaning. Aground is not about moving fast. It is about stopping in the wrong place.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. «Enemy ships ran aground in the shallow lagoon» still makes sense if you say «enemy ships got stuck in the shallow water». If your meaning fits, you are right.
fact: Aground means stuck on sand, rocks or the sea bottom in shallow water.
fact: Aground is used about ships and boats, in stories, news and history.
fact: «Ran aground» tells the reader that a ship is stuck, and maybe in danger.
fact: Aground can come after a verb, as in «was aground» or «went aground».
exercise: «The ship ran aground on the sandbank.» What does aground mean here? | Sailing very fast across the open sea | Tied up safely in the harbour | Sunk to the bottom of the deep ocean | *Stuck on sand in shallow water
exercise: In «The barge runs aground (gets stuck on the bottom) on the other side of the canal», which words explain aground? | on the other side of the canal | *gets stuck on the bottom | the barge | runs
exercise: «Enemy ships ran aground in the shallow lagoon.» Which meaning fits best when you swap it in? | *got stuck in shallow water | flew above the water | raced past the islands | were painted bright blue
test: «The boat went aground on the rocks, and the sailors shouted for help.» What does went aground mean? | *Got stuck on the rocks in shallow water | Sank in the deep sea | Was sold at the market | Sailed smoothly into the harbour
test: Which sentence uses aground correctly? | *The ferry was aground on a sandbank | The cat was aground on the sofa | Dad ran aground to the shop | The bus ran aground on the motorway
exam: In «The boat was aground for hours», the word aground is a | *adjective | noun | verb | full stop
